WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:07.040 align:start position:19%
So, what do we actually lose?
This is the part that hurts. Of the

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:11.040 align:start position:19%
roughly 1,200 known ancient Greek
authors, we have complete works from

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:17.040 align:start position:19%
only a handful. Aeschylus wrote around
90 plays, seven survived. Sophocles

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:22.200 align:start position:19%
wrote about 120, seven survived.
Euripides, 90 plays, 19 survived.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:26.720 align:start position:19%
Sappho, the most famous poet of the
ancient world, survives only in

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.760 align:start position:19%
fragments, a single complete poem.
Entire schools of philosophy, history,

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:35.240 align:start position:19%
scientific treatises, we know them only
as titles in other people's footnotes.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.360 align:start position:19%
Now, think about this. If even one more
warehouse of scrolls has survived, our

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.880 align:start position:19%
picture of the ancient world could be
completely different. The library's real

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:45.520 align:start position:19%
tragedy is not what burned, it is what
faded.

